1
00:00:00,288 --> 00:00:03,878
(Q31) Right, everyone, let’s make a start.

2
00:00:03,878 --> 00:00:22,001
Over the past few sessions, we’ve been considering the reasons why some world languages are in decline, and today I’m going to introduce another factor that affects languages, and the speakers of those languages, and that’s technology and, in particular, digital technology.

3
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:36,942
[Q31答案句：321000， around=approximately adv.大约] In order to illustrate its effect, I’m going to focus on the Icelandic language, which is spoken by around 321,000 people, most of whom live in Iceland – an island in the North Atlantic Ocean.

4
00:00:36,942 --> 00:00:42,723
(Q32) The problem for this language is not the number of speakers – even though this number is small.

5
00:00:42,723 --> 00:00:52,641
[Q32答案句+Q32干扰项：... is not ...,  nor..., In fact 表示转折！] Nor is it about losing words to other languages, such as English. In fact, the vocabulary of Icelandic is continually increasing&nbsp;

6
00:00:52,641 --> 00:00:59,684
because when speakers need a new word for something, they tend to create one, rather than borrowing from another language.

7
00:00:59,684 --> 00:01:12,979
(Q33) All this makes Icelandic quite a special language – it’s changed very little in the past millennium, yet it can handle twenty-first-century concepts related to the use of computers and digital technology.

8
00:01:12,979 --> 00:01:21,339
Take, for example, the word for web browser … this is vafri in Icelandic, which comes from the verb ‘to wander’.

9
00:01:21,339 --> 00:01:27,621
I can’t think of a more appropriate term because that’s exactly what you do mentally when you browse the internet.

10
00:01:27,621 --> 00:01:34,504
[Q33答案句： podcast， there's another = and, 并列递进关系] Then there’s an Icelandic word for podcast – which is too hard to pronounce! And so on.

11
00:01:34,504 --> 00:01:45,548
(Q34) [digital出现] Icelandic, then, is alive and growing, but – and it’s a big but – young Icelanders spend a great deal of time in the digital world and this world is predominantly English.

12
00:01:45,548 --> 00:01:48,281
[Q34答案句： think about 也可以是一种举例] Think about smartphones.

13
00:01:48,281 --> 00:02:00,747
(Q35) They didn’t even exist until comparatively recently, but today young people use them all the time to read books, watch TV or films, play games, listen to music, and so on.

14
00:02:00,747 --> 00:02:14,612
[Q35答案句：听前预测， 寻找adj类] Obviously, this is a good thing in many respects because it promotes their bilingual skills, but the extent of the influence of English in the virtual world is staggering and it’s all happening really fast.

15
00:02:14,612 --> 00:02:21,835
(Q36) For their parents and grandparents, the change is less concerning because they already have their native-speaker skills in Icelandic.

16
00:02:21,835 --> 00:02:26,390
But for young speakers – well, the outcome is a little troubling.

17
00:02:26,390 --> 00:02:35,176
[Q36答案句： conversation = discussion， 扩展记忆区间 playground discussion] For example, teachers have found that playground conversations in Icelandic secondary schools can be conducted entirely in English,&nbsp;

18
00:02:35,176 --> 00:02:44,811
(Q37) [Q37答案句：picture，find = identify,     what is in a ... = content] while teachers of much younger children have reported situations where their classes find it easier to say what is in a picture using English, rather than Icelandic.

19
00:02:44,811 --> 00:02:57,836
(Q38) The very real and worrying consequence of all this is that the young generation in Iceland is at risk of losing its mother tongue.

20
00:02:57,836 --> 00:03:16,248
[跟住定位词：  has a population the size of = number of ...] Of course, this is happening to other European languages too, but while internet companies might be willing to offer, say, French options in their systems, it’s much harder for them to justify the expense of doing the same for a language that has a population the size of a French town, such as Nice.

21
00:03:16,248 --> 00:03:23,732
[Q38答案句：grammar + 扩展记忆区间] The other drawback of Icelandic is the grammar, which is significantly more complex than in most languages.

22
00:03:23,732 --> 00:03:28,889
At the moment, the tech giants are simply not interested in tackling this.

23
00:03:28,889 --> 00:03:33,208
(Q39) So, what is the Icelandic government doing about this?

24
00:03:33,208 --> 00:03:48,681
Well, large sums of money are being allocated to a language technology fund that it is hoped will lead to the development of Icelandic sourced apps and other social media and digital systems, but clearly this is going to be an uphill struggle.

25
00:03:48,681 --> 00:03:55,363
[跟住定位词： positive side 讲的是好事 = believes ...区分态度] On the positive side, they know that Icelandic is still the official language of education and government.

26
00:03:55,363 --> 00:04:05,694
[跟住定位词： sound future, future 原词， sound=secure  可靠的] It has survived for well over a thousand years and the experts predict that its future in this nation state is sound and will continue to be so.

27
00:04:05,694 --> 00:04:11,782
However, there’s no doubt that it’s becoming an inevitable second choice in young people’s lives.

28
00:04:11,782 --> 00:04:15,072
This raises important questions.

29
00:04:15,072 --> 00:04:22,942
[Q39答案句： identity] When you consider how much of the past is tied up in a language, will young Icelanders lose their sense of their own identity?

30
00:04:22,942 --> 00:04:26,405
(Q40) Another issue that concerns the government of Iceland is this.

31
00:04:26,405 --> 00:04:28,905
[Q40答案句： children出现， neither表否定=not， 预测为adj] If children are learning two languages through different routes, neither of which they are fully fluent in, will they be able to express themselves properly?

